# Extra Instructions

Rules the LLM follows when it writes SQL for `listings`.

- `price` is the nightly price in U.S. dollars. When the user asks what something costs, use `price` and round money to whole dollars in the answer.
- The `host_since` and `instant_bookable` columns are entirely empty (`NULL`) for every row in this dataset. If a user asks about host tenure or instant booking, say plainly that this data isn't available rather than running a query that will silently return nothing.
- `review_scores_rating` and `reviews_per_month` are missing for about 1,761 of the 14,887 listings that have no reviews yet. When a question depends on either column, mention that these listings are excluded, so the reader knows the answer isn't based on all 14,887 listings.
- When comparing prices, ratings, or any other metric across cities or neighbourhoods, state in plain language which one is highest and which is lowest — don't just return a table and stop. Also name which of the three cities (Chicago, Columbus, or Twin Cities) the answer covers, since neighbourhood names can repeat or sound similar across metro areas.



